<p>Here is a number that should bother every business owner: 80 percent of sales require at least five follow-ups, but 44 percent of salespeople give up after just one. That gap is where money goes to die.</p>

<p>If you are a small business owner doing your own follow-up, the numbers are even worse. You are busy delivering the service, managing the team, handling admin. Following up with a lead from last Tuesday is the thing that always gets pushed to tomorrow. And by the time tomorrow arrives, that lead has already booked with someone who responded faster.</p>

<p>The solution is not to be more disciplined about follow-up. It is to remove yourself from the process entirely. Build a system that follows up automatically, on the right schedule, through the right channels, with the right message. Then let it run.</p>

<div class="divider">...</div>

<h2>Why follow-up is where most businesses lose money</h2>

<p>Think about what it costs to get a lead in the first place. You have paid for the ad, built the website, optimised your Google listing, asked for referrals. Every lead that walks through the door represents time and money already spent. When you fail to follow up, you are not just missing a sale. You are wasting the investment that brought that lead to you.</p>

<p>The maths is simple. If you generate 50 leads a month and convert 10 of them (a 20 percent conversion rate), improving your follow-up to convert just 5 more pushes your revenue up by 50 percent. Same traffic. Same spend. Better follow-up.</p>

<p>Most leads do not buy on the first contact. They are interested but not ready. They need more information, more trust, or simply more time. A follow-up system keeps you present during that decision-making window so that when they are ready, you are the business they think of.</p>

<blockquote><p>The fortune is in the follow-up. Not because the phrase is catchy, but because it is mathematically true.</p></blockquote>

<h2>The follow-up timeline</h2>

<p>Timing is everything. Follow up too fast and you feel pushy. Wait too long and they have forgotten you. Here is a timeline that works for most service businesses.</p>

<p><strong>Immediately (within 5 minutes).</strong> The moment someone fills out a form, calls your number, or sends an enquiry, they should receive an instant response. An automated SMS saying "Thanks for reaching out. We have received your message and will be in touch shortly" does two things. It confirms their enquiry went through, and it sets the expectation that you are responsive. Pair this with an email that provides a bit more detail about what happens next.</p>

<p><strong>Within 24 hours.</strong> If the lead has not converted after the initial contact, send a follow-up that adds value. This is not "just checking in." It is a message that gives them something useful. A link to a relevant page on your site, a quick answer to a common question, or a case study showing how you helped someone in a similar situation.</p>

<p><strong>Day 3.</strong> A brief, friendly message. Something like "I know things get busy. Just wanted to make sure you have everything you need to make a decision. Happy to answer any questions." This is the message that catches people who were interested but got distracted by life.</p>

<p><strong>Day 7.</strong> If they still have not responded, try a different channel. If your first messages were emails, send an SMS. If you started with SMS, try email. Some people simply prefer one over the other, and switching channels can be the nudge that gets a response.</p>

<p><strong>Day 30.</strong> A longer gap here is intentional. This message acknowledges that the timing may not have been right and leaves the door open. "No pressure at all. If your situation changes or you would like to revisit this down the track, we are here." This is about staying in their memory, not pushing for a sale.</p>

