<h2>Email vs. SMS at each stage</h2>

<p>Email and SMS serve different purposes, and using both strategically gives you the best chance of being seen.</p>

<p><strong>SMS is for urgency and brevity.</strong> Use it for the immediate confirmation, for short check-ins, and for time-sensitive messages. <a href="/blog/sms-marketing-small-business">SMS open rates sit around 98 percent</a>. Almost everyone reads a text message within minutes of receiving it. But keep your texts short. Two or three sentences at most. No one wants to read a novel in their messaging app.</p>

<p><strong><a href="/blog/email-marketing-small-business">Email</a> is for depth and value.</strong> Use it when you need to share links, explain your process, include testimonials, or provide detailed information. Email gives you space to build trust through content. The open rate is lower (20 to 30 percent on average), but the people who open are engaged enough to read.</p>

<p>A good follow-up sequence uses both. Immediate response via SMS. Detailed follow-up via email within 24 hours. Day 3 check-in via SMS. Day 7 value-add via email. Day 30 re-engagement via email. This rhythm ensures you appear in both their inbox and their messages without overwhelming either channel.</p>

<h2>Personalisation that actually matters</h2>

<p>Personalisation does not mean inserting someone's first name into a template (though you should do that too). Real personalisation is about relevance. It means the message addresses their specific situation, not a generic audience.</p>

<p>Here is how to personalise at scale without writing individual messages:</p>

<ul>
  <li><strong>Segment by service interest.</strong> If someone enquired about web design, they should not receive follow-up content about social media management. Tag leads by the service they asked about and route them into the appropriate sequence.</li>
  <li><strong>Reference their action.</strong> "You checked out our pricing page last week" or "You downloaded our guide on kitchen renovations" shows that you are paying attention, not just blasting everyone with the same message.</li>
  <li><strong>Match the tone to the stage.</strong> Early messages should be warm and helpful. Later messages can be more direct. The person who submitted a form yesterday is in a different headspace than the person you are re-engaging after a month of silence.</li>
</ul>

<p>This level of personalisation is entirely achievable with automation. You set it up once, and the system handles the routing and messaging from that point forward.</p>

<blockquote><p>Automation does not make your communication feel robotic. Bad automation does. Good automation feels like attentive service.</p></blockquote>

